# 14 Body and interfacial forces: the acceleration event {#ch:acceleration}

## Forces live on faces

All forces other than pressure and viscosity are collected in the face field
$\mathbf{a}$ (`a`). At the start of each step the `viscous_term` event
resets it to zero; the `acceleration` event fills it; and the last
body of the `acceleration` event, in `centered.h`, builds the
provisional face velocity
$$
u_f^\star = f_m\left(\overline{u} + \Delta t\,a\right),\qquad
\overline{u}_{i-\frac 12}= \tfrac12(u_{i-1} + u_i).
\qquad (14.1)
$$

> **In the code: `centered.h`, event `acceleration`**
>
> ~~~literatec
> foreach_face()
>   uf.x[] = fm.x[]*(face_value (u.x, 0) + dt*a.x[]);
> ~~~

## Who writes into $\mathbf{a}$, and in which order

Several files overload `acceleration`. A small test compiled for this
document confirmed that Basilisk runs overloaded events in *reverse*
order of definition: the body defined last runs first. For a program that
includes `centered.h`, `two-phase.h`, `tension.h` and
`reduced.h`, then defines its own `acceleration` event, the order
within the slot is:

1.  the `acceleration` event in your file, which can add any body
    force to $\mathbf{a}$ ([section 14.8](#sec:userforce));

2.  `reduced.h`, which computes the gravity potential $\phi$ of
    [(8.8)](08_equations) in interfacial cells;

3.  `tension.h`, which adds $\sigma\kappa$ to $\phi$ (only if
    `f.sigma` is set);

4.  `iforce.h`, which turns $\phi$ into the face acceleration
    $\alpha\phi\nabla f$ and adds it to $\mathbf{a}$;

5.  `centered.h`, which builds $u_f^\star$ with (14.1).

This is why `iforce.h` is included before the modules that define
potentials: being defined earlier, it runs after them.

## The interfacial force

`iforce.h` represents any force concentrated on the interface and
normal to it, $\phi\,\mathbf{n}\,\delta_s$, by $\phi\nabla f$ ([section 8.4](08_equations#sec:reduced-derivation)).
On the face between cells $i-1$ and $i$:
$$
a_{i-\frac 12}\mathrel{+}= \frac{\alpha_{i-\frac 12}}{f_{m,{i-\frac 12}}}\;\phi_{i-\frac 12}\;\frac{f_i - f_{i-1}}{\Delta},
\qquad \phi_{i-\frac 12}= \tfrac12(\phi_{i-1} + \phi_i),
\qquad (14.2)
$$
where $\alpha/f_m = 1/\rho$ on the face. $\phi$ is only defined in cells that
contain the interface; if only one of the two cells has a value, that value
is used. The term is nonzero only where $f$ changes across the face, so the
force acts in a band one or two cells thick around the interface.

> **In the code: `iforce.h`**
>
> ~~~literatec
> if (f[] != f[-1] && fm.x[] > 0.) {
>   double phif = (phi[] < nodata && phi[-1] < nodata) ? (phi[] + phi[-1])/2. : ...;
>   ia.x[] += alpha.x[]/(fm.x[] + SEPS)*phif*(f[] - f[-1])/Delta;
> }
> ~~~

## Gravity with reduced.h

`reduced.h` sets, in each interfacial cell,
$$
\phi = (\rho_2 - \rho_1)\,\mathbf{G}\cdot(\mathbf{x}_\Gamma - \mathbf{Z}),
$$
where $\mathbf{x}_\Gamma$ is the position of the interface in that cell, computed
by `position()` from height functions ([section 14.6](#sec:heights)). With
$\mathbf{G} = (0,-g,0)$, $\mathbf{Z} = 0$ and a free surface at elevation
$\eta$, $\phi = (\rho_1 - \rho_2)\,g\,\eta$.

The physical meaning follows from [section 8.4](08_equations#sec:reduced-derivation): the solver
works with the dynamic pressure $p' = p - \rho\,\mathbf{G}\cdot\mathbf{x}$. In
still water with a flat surface at $y = 0$, $p'$ is the same constant in both
fluids and $\phi = 0$, so nothing moves. Under a wave crest of height $\eta$,
$p'$ in the water just below the surface is higher than in the air by
$(\rho_1 - \rho_2)g\eta$: the weight of the extra water column. The
interfacial force is what creates this jump.

## Balanced force: why $\mathbf{a}$ must sit on faces {#sec:balanced}

Consider a fluid at rest with a force $\phi\nabla f$ and constant $\phi$
(a static drop with surface tension, or hydrostatic balance). The exact
solution is $\mathbf{u}= 0$ with $p = \phi f + \text{const}$: the pressure jump
balances the force. In the projection ([chapter 15](15_projection)) the face
velocity becomes
$$
u_f^{n+1} = \Delta t\,\alpha\left(\phi\,\frac{f_i - f_{i-1}}{\Delta} - \frac{p_i - p_{i-1}}{\Delta}\right).
$$
Because the force and the pressure gradient use the same two cells and the
same weight $\alpha$, the discrete pressure $p_i = \phi f_i$ makes this zero
on every face, exactly. If instead the force were computed at cell centres
and then interpolated, the two terms would use different stencils, their
difference would not vanish, and the solver would create *spurious
currents* at the interface every step. Figure 14.1 shows the
arrangement.

![Figure 14.1: Balanced-force arrangement. On each face the interfacial force
(red) and the pressure gradient (blue) are both differences of the same two
cell values, weighted by the same $\alpha$, so they can cancel exactly.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_balanced.png)

On an adaptive mesh the same reasoning requires $f$ and $p$ to be
interpolated the same way at fine/coarse faces, so `iforce.h`
temporarily gives $f$ the prolongation function of $p$ while computing
(14.2).

## Interface position and curvature from height functions {#sec:heights}

Both `position()` (gravity) and `curvature()` (surface tension) use
*height functions*. For an interface that is closer to horizontal than
vertical, sum the volume fractions in a vertical column of cells:
$$
h_i = \Delta\sum_{k} f_{i,k},
$$
which is the height of the water in column $i$ measured from the bottom of
the stencil (figure 14.2). `heights.h` integrates half-columns
up and down from each cell, accepts the height only if the column reaches a
full cell at one end and an empty cell at the other (so it crosses the
interface once), and gives up if the interface is farther than 5.5 cells. Differencing neighbouring heights gives the slope and
curvature:
$$
h_x = \frac{h_{i+1} - h_{i-1}}{2\Delta},\quad
h_{xx} = \frac{h_{i+1} - 2h_i + h_{i-1}}{\Delta^2},\quad
\kappa = \frac{h_{xx}}{(1 + h_x^2)^{3/2}}
$$
in 2D, and
$$
\kappa = \frac{h_{xx}(1 + h_y^2) + h_{yy}(1 + h_x^2) - 2h_{xy}h_xh_y}{(1 + h_x^2 + h_y^2)^{3/2}}
$$
in 3D. The heights are second-order accurate and the curvature converges
with mesh refinement, which plain derivatives of $f$ do not. Where heights
are not available (very curved or under-resolved interfaces), Basilisk falls
back on fitting a parabola (paraboloid in 3D) to interface positions.

![Figure 14.2: Height function. Summing $f$ down the outlined column gives the
height of the interface in that column. Neighbouring columns give the slope
and curvature.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_heights.png)

## Surface tension

`tension.h` sets $\phi = \sigma\kappa$ in interfacial cells, so
(14.2) gives the continuum surface force
$\sigma\kappa\nabla f/\rho$. The scheme is explicit and needs the capillary
time step of [chapter 10](10_timestep). It is switched on by giving the volume
fraction a coefficient, `f.sigma = 72.8e-3;` (SI, water--air). Whether it
matters is decided by the Bond and Weber numbers ([section 7.9](07_cfd_concepts#sec:dimless)).

## Adding your own body force {#sec:userforce}

Any extra acceleration goes into $\mathbf{a}$ in an `acceleration` event of
your own. Two rules follow from the balanced-force argument above: write the
force on faces (component `x` on `x` faces, and so on), and
multiply nothing by $f_m$ yourself, since `centered.h` does that when it
builds $u_f^\star$. A uniform gravity without `reduced.h`, as in
`$BASILISK/test/rising.c`:

~~~literatec
event acceleration (i++) {
  face vector av = a;
  foreach_face(x)
    av.x[] -= 0.98;
}
~~~

A damping (sponge) layer that relaxes the velocity to zero at rate
$\sigma(x)$, integrated implicitly so that it is stable for any
$\sigma\Delta t$ ([section 20.2](20_zones#sec:sponge)):

~~~literatec
event acceleration (i++) {
  face vector av = a;
  foreach_face()
    if (sigma(x) > 0.)
      av.x[] -= sigma(x)*uf.x[]/(1. + dt*sigma(x));
}
~~~

For a single-phase flow, uniform gravity does not need to be added at all:
it is balanced by a hydrostatic pressure and only changes $p$.

